var e={id:`sysdesign-l0-storage-gateway-ratelimit`,subjectId:`sysdesign`,level:0,title:`Blob Storage, CDNs, API Gateways & Rate Limiting`,whyItMatters:`Two answers separate a senior candidate from a memorized one: "the client uploads straight to object storage with a pre-signed URL, not through my app server" and "the limit is a token bucket in Redis, because ten app instances with local counters enforce ten times the limit." Both live in this module. It is also where the money lives — cache-hit ratio and storage class decide your bill more than any algorithm you will write.`,estMinutes:55,sections:[{type:`intuition`,title:`Your app servers should move decisions, not bytes`,md:`A restaurant kitchen that also personally drives every delivery stops cooking.

- Application servers exist to make **decisions**: authorize, validate, write a row, choose what to show.
- Bytes are not decisions. A 50 MB video, a 4 MB product photo, a nightly export — that is freight.
- This module is the freight infrastructure: **object storage** (where bytes live), the **CDN** (where bytes are served from), and the **API gateway** (the door every request enters).
- Plus the doorman on that door: **rate limiting** — the rule deciding how many requests each caller gets.
- One theme connects all four: push work outward to the edge, and keep your app servers doing the one thing only they can do.`},{type:`intuition`,title:`Blob storage: a valet garage for bytes`,md:`You hand over the car and get a ticket. You never walk in and rearrange the garage yourself.

- A **blob / object store** (S3, GCS, Azure Blob) holds immutable byte blobs, each addressed by a **key** — a plain string like \`videos/2026/9f3c.mp4\`.
- **Cheap**: roughly $0.023 per GB-month for hot storage. A terabyte is about $23/month; a comparable attached disk (EBS) is 4–5× that and needs capacity planning.
- **Effectively unlimited**: no resizing, no "disk full" page at 3am. Objects up to 5 TB each, and the bucket has no size limit at all.
- **Durable by replication**: every object is written to at least three separate availability zones before the PUT is acknowledged. S3 advertises eleven nines of durability (99.999999999%) — that is replica arithmetic against disk failure, not a promise about your bad DELETE.
- **HTTP-addressable**: every object has a URL. A browser, a CDN, or a Spark job reads it directly — no driver, no connection pool, no mount.`},{type:`intuition`,title:`What it is NOT: an object store is not a filesystem`,md:"The console draws folders. There are no folders. Every design that forgets this pays for it later.\n\n- **No directories.** The key `a/b/c.jpg` is one flat string; the slashes are a display convention. There is no folder object to open, lock, or move.\n- **Rename is a copy.** Moving `a/x.mp4` to `b/x.mp4` means read all the bytes, write them at the new key, delete the old. On a 5 GB object that is a 5 GB job, not a metadata edit.\n- **No partial append, no in-place edit.** You cannot append a line to a log or patch byte 400 — you replace the whole object. (Multipart upload assembles ONE new object from parts; it is not appending to an existing one.)\n- **`list` is a query, not a directory read.** Listing a prefix scans a key index and pages ~1000 keys at a time. A bucket with 50M objects makes `list` slow and expensive — design it out of hot paths, and keep the index you actually query in a database.\n- **Latency is network-shaped**: tens of milliseconds to first byte, not microseconds. Treat it as a remote service, because it is one.\n- When NOT to use it: small random reads and writes, POSIX semantics, locking, anything transactional. That is a database or a real filesystem."},{type:`note`,md:"**The standard data model: metadata in the database, bytes in the blob store.** Never store a 50 MB video in a BLOB column. The row holds what you query, filter, and join on — `id`, `owner_id`, `title`, `size_bytes`, `content_type`, `status`, `created_at`, and the storage **key**; the object store holds the bytes at that key. Why this split wins: your database stays small enough to cache in memory, backups stay fast, replication does not ship gigabytes of video to every follower, and byte reads never touch the database at all. The price you accept is that **there is no transaction across the two** — a row can exist with no object (abandoned upload) or an object with no row (row deleted first). Real systems handle it with a `status` column plus a janitor job that reconciles both directions. Say that out loud in an interview; it is the half most candidates skip."},{type:`intuition`,title:`The pattern interviewers are waiting for: pre-signed uploads`,md:`Lead with the one-line version: **do not proxy uploads through your application server.**

- The naive design: the browser POSTs the file to your API, your API buffers it, then uploads it to the object store. Every byte crosses your app tier **twice** — in, then back out.
- Do the arithmetic. 1000 users uploading a 50 MB video over 5 minutes = 50 GB / 300 s ≈ 167 MB/s inbound ≈ **1.3 Gbps in and 1.3 Gbps out** on the app tier, plus memory or disk to buffer it. Your app servers are now a bandwidth appliance that also happens to run business logic.
- The fix: the client asks your API for a **pre-signed URL** — an ordinary storage URL with an expiry and a signature in the query string. Possession of that URL *is* the permission, and it is scoped to one bucket, one key, one method, one short window.
- Your API's work per upload collapses to one ~400-byte JSON response: check auth and quota, generate the key, INSERT a \`pending\` row, sign the URL. That scales to millions of uploads on a handful of boxes.
- Then storage **fires an event** on object-created → a queue → a worker that transcodes, thumbnails, virus-scans, and flips the row to \`ready\`. Upload and processing are now decoupled and scale independently.
- The same trick works downward: a pre-signed GET (or a CDN signed URL) hands private bytes to a client without streaming them through your app.`},{type:`visual`,component:`PointerBoxDiagram`,props:{title:`Pre-signed upload: how the bytes route around your app`,notice:`Frame 1 is the naive version — watch what the app server is holding. Then watch the same upload with a pre-signed URL.`,leftLabel:`who is acting`,rightLabel:`what holds the bytes`,frames:[{note:`NAIVE: the browser POSTs 50 MB to your API, which buffers it and re-uploads it to storage. Every byte crosses the app tier twice. At 1000 concurrent uploads that is ~1.3 Gbps in plus ~1.3 Gbps out — your business-logic servers are now a bandwidth appliance, and adding CPU does not fix a saturated NIC.`,stack:[{name:`browser: POST /upload (50 MB)`,to:`app`,danger:!0}],heap:[{id:`app`,value:`~2.6 Gbps through one NIC + buffers`,label:`app server — every byte, twice`,danger:!0},{id:`store`,value:`object store, idle`,label:`could have taken the bytes directly`}]},{note:`FIXED, step 1: the browser asks for permission, not for a pipe. The API checks auth and quota, picks the key, INSERTs a row with status=pending, and signs a URL scoped to that one key, method PUT, expiring in 15 minutes. Response: ~400 bytes.`,stack:[{name:`browser: POST /uploads (400 B)`,to:`app`}],heap:[{id:`app`,value:`auth + quota, INSERT status=pending`,label:`app server — decisions only`},{id:`url`,value:`PUT https://bucket.s3/../9f3c.mp4?Expires=900&Signature=..`,label:`pre-signed URL — the permission IS the URL`}]},{note:`Step 2: the browser PUTs 50 MB straight to object storage. The object store verifies the signature itself — it does not call your API. Your app tier handled zero video bytes and is free to serve other requests.`,stack:[{name:`browser: PUT 50 MB`,to:`store`}],heap:[{id:`app`,value:`0 bytes of video handled`,label:`app server — not in the data path`},{id:`store`,value:`videos/9f3c.mp4 (50 MB) stored`,label:`object store — verifies the signature locally`}]},{note:`Step 3: storage emits an object-created event into a queue. This is the decoupling seam — a launch-day spike piles up in the queue instead of melting the workers, and a failed job is retried instead of lost.`,stack:[{name:`storage: ObjectCreated event`,to:`queue`}],heap:[{id:`store`,value:`videos/9f3c.mp4`,label:`object store`},{id:`queue`,value:`1 message: bucket, key, size`,label:`queue — absorbs spikes, retries failures`}]},{note:`Step 4: a worker consumes the message, transcodes and scans, then flips the row to status=ready. The database only ever held metadata; the CDN now serves the bytes from the object store. Four hops, and your API touched none of the freight.`,stack:[{name:`worker: consume message`,to:`queue`},{name:`worker: UPDATE videos`,to:`db`}],heap:[{id:`queue`,value:`empty — drained`,label:`queue`},{id:`db`,value:`id=9f3c, status=ready, key=videos/9f3c.mp4`,label:`database — metadata only, never bytes`}]}]}},{type:`note`,md:`**Storage classes and lifecycle rules.** Not all bytes are equally hot. Standard (~$0.023/GB-month) is for objects read often; infrequent-access tiers run ~$0.0125/GB-month but add a per-GB retrieval fee; archive tiers (Glacier and friends) drop to ~$0.001–0.004/GB-month — 20× cheaper — at the cost of minutes-to-hours restore times and real retrieval charges. A **lifecycle rule** is a declarative policy on the bucket: "after 30 days move to infrequent access, after 90 days archive, after 7 years delete." Nobody writes a cron job for this. Two traps: archive tiers have minimum storage durations (often 90–180 days), so churning short-lived objects through them costs *more* than standard; and **egress**, not storage, is usually the biggest line on the bill — moving 100 TB/month out at ~$0.09/GB is ~$9,000, which is the entire money argument for the CDN in the next section.`},{type:`intuition`,title:`CDN: warehouses instead of one factory`,md:`The factory (your origin) is in Virginia. The warehouse is 20 km from the customer.

- A **CDN** is a fleet of **PoPs** (points of presence) — cache servers in hundreds of cities. DNS or anycast routing sends each user to a nearby one.
- The win is physics. Light in fiber covers ~200 km/ms and real paths are worse: Virginia→Mumbai is ~200 ms round trip, a Mumbai PoP is ~5 ms. On a page pulling 40 assets that gap is seconds, not milliseconds.
- The metric is **cache-hit ratio**: the fraction of requests the edge answers without asking your origin. Quote it whenever an interviewer asks how you would cut origin load or bandwidth spend.
- Run the numbers: 10,000 req/s at the edge with a 95% hit ratio leaves the origin serving 500/s. Let the hit ratio slip to 70% and the origin gets 3,000/s — a **6× load spike caused by a config change, not by traffic**.
- That sensitivity is why the next section is about cache keys. Hit ratio is not something you hope for; it is something you configure.`},{type:`intuition`,title:`The cache key — and how one query parameter destroys your hit rate`,md:'The **cache key** is what a PoP hashes to decide "do I already have this?" — by default host + path + the *full* query string.\n\n- That default is a trap. `/logo.png?utm_source=twitter` and `/logo.png?utm_source=email` are two keys, two objects, two origin fetches. One image becomes N cache entries, all cold.\n- Ten tracking parameters with a few values each can shatter one asset into thousands of entries. Nothing about the bytes changed; your marketing team just invented campaign tags.\n- The fix is explicit key configuration: strip or whitelist query parameters, normalize case and trailing slashes, and only **`Vary`** on headers you genuinely serve differently. `Vary: Accept-Encoding` is fine; `Vary: User-Agent` fragments your cache by browser version and is almost always a mistake.\n- **TTL** (`Cache-Control: max-age`) is how long an edge may serve a copy without revalidating. Long TTL → high hit ratio and stale content; short TTL → the reverse. It is the same dial as any cache.\n- **Purge / invalidation** exists but is slow and rate-limited — it must reach every PoP, taking seconds to minutes. Never design a system whose *correctness* depends on a fast global purge.\n- The better answer is **versioned URLs**: serve `/app.9f3c2a.js` with a one-year TTL and `immutable`, and let a deploy publish a new filename. Nothing to invalidate, ever. Keep purge for genuine emergencies — leaked content, a wrong price.'},{type:`intuition`,title:`Push vs pull, private content, and what a CDN buys beyond speed`,md:`- **Pull CDN** (the default): edges start empty; the first request for an object at a PoP misses and fetches from origin, then caches it. Self-managing — but every PoP pays its own cold miss, and a new PoP or a mass purge sends a burst at your origin.
- **Push CDN**: you upload objects to the CDN ahead of time. Worth it for a small, known, high-value set — a game patch, a launch-day video — where cold misses or an origin thundering herd are unacceptable. The cost is operational: you own distribution and cleanup.
- **Signed URLs / signed cookies** put private content at the edge: the URL carries an expiry and a signature the PoP validates locally. A paid course video is cached near the user and still not publicly shareable, and your origin is never consulted for authorization.
- **Origin offload** is the underrated benefit. At a 95% hit ratio your origin fleet *and* your egress bill both shrink ~20×, and CDN egress is cheaper per GB than cloud egress to begin with.
- **DDoS absorption**: a CDN edge has terabits of capacity spread across hundreds of PoPs, so a volumetric flood dissipates there instead of at your single-region origin. TLS termination and WAF rules usually live at the same edge.
- Summary line for interviews: a CDN buys latency, origin capacity, bandwidth cost, and a security perimeter — four benefits, one component.`},{type:`note`,md:"**Be honest: a CDN is a caching layer, so it inherits every problem from the caching module** — stale reads after an update, no strong consistency, best-effort invalidation — multiplied by hundreds of PoPs you cannot inspect. Three CDN-specific twists worth naming: (1) **negative caching** is brutal — an edge that caches a 404 or a 500 during a bad deploy keeps serving it after the fix lands, so set short TTLs on error responses; (2) **never cache a personalized response under a shared key** — one `Cache-Control: public` on a page containing a logged-in user's name serves that user's page to strangers, a classic production incident; (3) a cold cache after a mass purge is a **thundering herd against your origin**, at continental scale — which is why versioned URLs beat purging."},{type:`intuition`,title:`API gateway: one front door instead of thirty`,md:`Thirty microservices each doing their own authentication is thirty implementations and thirty chances to get it wrong.

- **Routing**: one public hostname; the gateway maps \`/orders/*\` to the orders service and \`/users/*\` to the users service. Clients never learn your internal topology, so you can split a service without breaking them.
- **Authentication and authorization**: validate the JWT or API key once, at the door, and pass a trusted identity header inward. Services stop reimplementing token parsing.
- **Rate limiting and quotas**: enforced per API key before the request costs a service anything. (The whole second half of this module.)
- **Request/response transformation**: protocol bridging (public REST in, internal gRPC out), version shims that keep an old client alive, response field stripping.
- **API-key management**: issue, rotate, revoke; map each key to a plan and a quota. This is what turns a public API into a product you can bill for.
- **Metrics and logging**: one place that sees every request — latency percentiles, status codes, per-customer usage. Your SLO dashboard and your billing data both start here.`},{type:`note`,md:`**Raise the counter-argument yourself.** Centralizing *cross-cutting* concerns is right; centralizing *business* logic builds a **smart pipe**. Once teams start putting conditional routing, request rewrites, and response assembly into gateway config, the gateway becomes a shared, undertested deployment bottleneck — every team queues behind it, and its config is a codebase nobody owns. It is also a single point of failure and an extra network hop, so it must run redundantly and be watched like a service. The rule: **generic concerns in the gateway, product logic in services.** Two neighbouring terms: a **BFF** (backend-for-frontend) is a gateway per client type — a mobile BFF that aggregates five calls into one fat response, a web BFF that returns five thin ones — so one client's needs never distort the shared API. And **gateway vs service mesh** in one honest line: the gateway handles **north-south** traffic (requests entering your system from outside), a mesh handles **east-west** traffic (service-to-service inside) through sidecar proxies — different traffic, overlapping features, and large systems usually run both.`},{type:`intuition`,title:`Rate limiting: why it exists before how it works`,md:`A limiter is the bouncer at the door, not the bug fix inside. It exists because **one client can ruin the service for everyone else**.

- **Protect the backend.** A client with a retry loop and no backoff sends thousands of requests per second *by accident*. Without a limiter, your database — not the buggy client — is what falls over.
- **Enforce fairness across tenants.** On a shared platform, one heavy customer must not eat the capacity the other 999 paid for. That is the noisy-neighbour problem, and the limiter is the fix.
- **Stop abuse.** Credential stuffing, scraping, and enumeration all depend on volume. Five login attempts per minute per account makes brute force useless while inconveniencing nobody real.
- **Control cost.** When each request costs you money — an LLM call, a third-party API, terabytes of egress — the limiter is a spending cap. A "free tier" is a rate limit with a price list attached.
- Know what it is *not*: not load balancing, not autoscaling, not a circuit breaker. Those add capacity or protect you *from a dependency*. The limiter decides who gets in at all.`},{type:`intuition`,title:`Token bucket and leaky bucket`,md:`Picture a bucket filling with tokens at a steady drip. Every request must take one token or be turned away.

- **Token bucket** is the standard choice. Two numbers define it: **capacity** (how many tokens can accumulate) and **refill rate** (tokens added per second).
- Say the mapping out loud in interviews: **capacity is the burst allowance; refill rate is the sustained ceiling.** Capacity 100 with 10/s refill means "10 requests per second in the long run, but you may spend a saved-up burst of 100 at once."
- It matches how real clients behave — a page load fires 20 requests then goes quiet — so normal bursty traffic is not punished for being bursty.
- It is cheap: **two numbers per key** (token count, last-refill timestamp) with the refill computed lazily on read — \`tokens = min(capacity, tokens + elapsed * rate)\`. No background timer, O(1) memory and O(1) time.
- **Leaky bucket** is the opposite instinct: requests join a FIFO queue that drains at a fixed rate, so the *output* is perfectly smooth however spiky the input. Bursts are delayed rather than allowed, and dropped once the queue fills.
- Reach for leaky bucket only when the downstream genuinely cannot take a burst — a legacy mainframe, a serial device, a paid API with a hard per-second cap. Its costs are added latency and a queue you must size and monitor. Everywhere else: token bucket.`},{type:`visual`,component:`RateLimiterSim`,props:{}},{type:`note`,md:`**Find this in the simulator.** Defaults are capacity 10, refill 2/s. Watch **steady 3/s**: it eats the ten stored tokens, then rejects about one request per second forever — arrivals above the refill rate have nowhere to come from. Now drag refill up to 3.0 and the rejections stop entirely; you raised the *sustained ceiling* above the arrival rate. Then switch to **burst of 15** and drag refill all the way to 10/s — and 5 requests still bounce on every single burst. That is the lesson to carry into interviews: a burst arrives faster than any refill can help, so **capacity alone decides how big a burst survives**. Refill rate governs the long run; capacity governs the instant. Sizing a limiter means choosing both, and "bursts of 15 against a capacity of 10" is a config bug no refill rate will ever fix.`},{type:`code`,lang:`python`,title:`Token bucket, and exactly which requests in a burst survive`,code:`class TokenBucket:
    def __init__(self, capacity, refill_per_sec):
        self.capacity = capacity          # burst allowance
        self.rate = refill_per_sec        # sustained ceiling
        self.tokens = float(capacity)
        self.last = 0.0

    def allow(self, now):
        elapsed = now - self.last
        self.tokens = min(self.capacity, self.tokens + elapsed * self.rate)
        self.last = now
        if self.tokens >= 1:
            self.tokens -= 1
            return True
        return False


bucket = TokenBucket(capacity=5, refill_per_sec=2)
arrivals = [0.0]*8 + [1.0]*3 + [2.0, 2.5, 3.0, 3.5]   # burst, burst, trickle
for i, t in enumerate(arrivals, 1):
    ok = bucket.allow(t)
    print(f"req {i:2d}  t={t:4.1f}s  tokens_left={bucket.tokens:4.1f}  {'ACCEPT' if ok else 'REJECT 429'}")

# --- real output ---
# req  1  t= 0.0s  tokens_left= 4.0  ACCEPT
# req  2  t= 0.0s  tokens_left= 3.0  ACCEPT
# req  3  t= 0.0s  tokens_left= 2.0  ACCEPT
# req  4  t= 0.0s  tokens_left= 1.0  ACCEPT
# req  5  t= 0.0s  tokens_left= 0.0  ACCEPT
# req  6  t= 0.0s  tokens_left= 0.0  REJECT 429
# req  7  t= 0.0s  tokens_left= 0.0  REJECT 429
# req  8  t= 0.0s  tokens_left= 0.0  REJECT 429
# req  9  t= 1.0s  tokens_left= 1.0  ACCEPT
# req 10  t= 1.0s  tokens_left= 0.0  ACCEPT
# req 11  t= 1.0s  tokens_left= 0.0  REJECT 429
# req 12  t= 2.0s  tokens_left= 1.0  ACCEPT
# req 13  t= 2.5s  tokens_left= 1.0  ACCEPT
# req 14  t= 3.0s  tokens_left= 1.0  ACCEPT
# req 15  t= 3.5s  tokens_left= 1.0  ACCEPT`,annotations:{3:`Capacity is the BURST allowance — the most requests servable in one instant. Nothing but capacity decides that number.`,4:`Refill rate is the SUSTAINED ceiling — the long-run requests per second this caller gets.`,10:`Lazy refill: no background timer, no cron job. Elapsed time x rate, clamped at capacity. Two numbers per key, O(1) per request — this is why token bucket is the default everywhere.`,19:`Arrival pattern: 8 requests in one instant, a 3-request burst one second later, then a trickle at exactly 2/s.`,30:`The bucket started full at 5, so exactly 5 of the 8 burst requests got through. Requests 6-8 arrive at the same instant with 0 tokens — refill rate cannot help inside a single instant, only capacity can.`,33:`One second later, refill added 2.0 tokens: requests 9 and 10 pass, request 11 bounces. The sustained ceiling asserting itself.`,36:`From here arrivals are 2/s — exactly the refill rate — so every one is accepted forever. Stay at or below the refill rate and you are never limited.`}},{type:`intuition`,title:`Fixed window, sliding log, sliding counter`,md:`Token bucket is not the only shape, and interviewers like to walk you up the ladder.

- **Fixed window counter**: one counter per (key, minute). Increment, reject above the limit, reset on the boundary. One integer with a TTL — the cheapest thing that works.
- Its flaw is the **boundary problem**. Limit 100/min: a client sends 100 at 12:00:59 and 100 more at 12:01:01. Both windows are legal, yet that is 200 requests in a 2-second span — **2× the limit** for any minute-long window straddling the boundary.
- **Sliding window log**: store a timestamp for every allowed request in a sorted set, drop entries older than the window, count what remains. Exact — no boundary artifact at all.
- Its flaw is memory: one timestamp per allowed request per key. A 1000 req/min limit across 1M users is up to a billion live timestamps, plus the cost of trimming the set on every single request.
- **Sliding window counter** — the practical approximation everyone actually ships. Keep the previous and current fixed-window counts and weight the previous one by how much of the sliding window still overlaps it.
- Two integers per key, no boundary cliff, and the only error is assuming the previous window's traffic was evenly spread — small, bounded, and fine in practice. This is what Cloudflare-class limiters run.`},{type:`math`,intro:`The boundary problem and the sliding-window fix, in numbers.`,latex:[`\\text{fixed window, limit } 100/\\text{min}: \\quad \\underbrace{100}_{\\text{at }12{:}00{:}59} + \\underbrace{100}_{\\text{at }12{:}01{:}01} = 200 \\text{ requests in } 2\\text{s}`,`\\text{sliding counter} \\;=\\; c_{\\text{prev}} \\cdot \\frac{\\text{overlap}}{\\text{window}} \\;+\\; c_{\\text{cur}}`,`\\text{at }12{:}01{:}15,\\ 60\\text{s window}: \\quad 80 \\cdot \\tfrac{45}{60} + 20 \\;=\\; 60 + 20 \\;=\\; 80 \\;\\le\\; 100 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\text{allow}`]},{type:`intuition`,title:`Where to enforce — and the distributed counter problem`,md:`Now the question that separates a real answer from a memorized one: **where does the counter live?**

- **At the edge / gateway**: rejects abusive traffic before it costs a service hop, and one policy covers every service. The right home for coarse per-API-key and per-IP limits.
- **Per service**: the service knows which of its own endpoints are expensive — search may deserve 10/s while a health check deserves 10,000/s. The right home for fine-grained, endpoint-aware limits.
- Real systems do both: a blunt limit at the door, a sharp one on the endpoint that actually hurts.
- **The distributed problem**: run 10 app instances, each with a local counter limiting to 100/min, and the client actually gets **1000/min**. The limit is silently multiplied by your instance count — and autoscaling to 40 instances quadruples it again without anyone changing a config.
- The naive fix, giving each instance limit/N, breaks the moment traffic is uneven (load balancers are not perfectly fair) or an instance dies — and it makes your published rate limit depend on your deployment size.
- The real fix: a **shared store** (Redis) with an **atomic** operation — \`INCR\` plus \`EXPIRE\`, or a small Lua script doing read-refill-decrement-write as one indivisible step. Without atomicity two instances read the same token count and both spend it: a lost update, and your limit leaks.`},{type:`note`,md:`**The tradeoff of a shared counter is a network hop on every single request.** An in-datacenter Redis call is ~0.5–1 ms: on a 20 ms endpoint that is a 5% latency tax, on a 2 ms endpoint it is 50% — measure before you shrug. Mitigations in the order you would try them: pipeline the limiter call with other Redis work; keep the limiter Redis in the same availability zone; shard the limiter by key so no node sees all traffic; or go **approximate** — each instance enforces a local bucket and syncs counts to the shared store every few hundred milliseconds, trading exactness for the round trip (this is what most very-large-scale limiters really do). Then answer the failure question before it is asked: if the limiter store is unreachable, do you **fail open** (allow, risking overload) or **fail closed** (reject, causing a self-inflicted outage)? For general rate limiting the usual answer is fail open with a loud alarm — the limiter must never be the thing that takes the site down — with fail-closed reserved for endpoints where abuse is worse than downtime, such as payments or login.`},{type:`note`,md:"**What to key on — the choice that decides whether the limiter is fair.** *User ID or API key* is the right default for authenticated traffic: it is the accountable unit, it survives the user changing networks, and it maps to the plan they pay for. *IP address* is all you have on anonymous endpoints (login, signup, password reset) — but **IP alone is wrong as a general policy**, because an entire office, university, or mobile carrier sits behind one NAT address: a limit tight enough to stop an attacker also blocks thousands of innocent users sharing that carrier-grade NAT, and a limit loose enough to spare them stops nobody. IPv6 inverts the problem — an attacker gets a whole /64 for free, so limit on the /64 prefix, not the single address. The practical answer is **layers**: per-user for fairness, per-IP (or per-prefix) for anonymous abuse, per-endpoint for the expensive routes, and a global limit protecting the backend's own capacity. One security note: when you must key on IP, take the client address from your proxy chain correctly — a blindly trusted `X-Forwarded-For` header makes the limiter bypassable with one line of curl."},{type:`intuition`,title:`The response contract, and degrading instead of slamming the door`,md:"A rejection is an API response. A well-designed one tells the client exactly how to behave next.\n\n- **429 Too Many Requests** is the status. Not 503 (that means *the server* is overloaded, not that *you* are over quota), and never a 200 with an error body.\n- **`Retry-After: 30`** — seconds, or an HTTP date, until the client may try again. That one header turns a guessing game into a schedule.\n- **`X-RateLimit-Limit` / `-Remaining` / `-Reset`** on *every* response, not only rejections, so a well-behaved client can self-pace and never see a 429. (An IETF draft standardizes these as `RateLimit-*`; the `X-` forms are what is deployed today.)\n- The client's duty is **exponential backoff with jitter**. Without jitter, every throttled client retries at the same instant the window resets — a synchronized **retry storm** that recreates the overload you just shed.\n- **Graceful degradation beats hard rejection when a human is waiting**: serve a stale cached response, a smaller result page, a lower-quality image, or accept the write and return **202 Accepted** with a job id. Hard-reject when the request is expensive and the caller is a machine that can wait.\n- The sentence to close on: the limiter's job is to shed load *predictably*, so the system degrades in an order you chose instead of collapsing in an order you did not."}],quiz:[{question:`Users upload 200 MB videos. Which design keeps your app tier from becoming a bandwidth bottleneck?`,options:[{text:`POST the file to your API, which streams it to the object store`,explanation:`Every byte still crosses your app tier twice — in and back out. Streaming avoids the memory buffer, not the bandwidth.`},{text:`POST the file to your API, which writes it to a shared NFS mount`,explanation:`Worse: same bandwidth through your app tier, plus a filesystem that now needs capacity planning and becomes its own single point of failure.`},{text:`The client asks your API for a pre-signed URL and PUTs the bytes directly to the object store`,explanation:`Correct. Your API returns ~400 bytes of JSON and handles zero video bytes; the object store validates the signature itself. Storage then fires an event to a queue for processing.`},{text:`Increase the app servers' CPU and add more instances`,explanation:`The bottleneck is the NIC, not the CPU. Scaling out to move freight is paying app-server prices for bandwidth the object store gives you directly.`}],correct:2},{question:"Marketing adds `?utm_source=...` to every shared link. Your CDN hit ratio collapses. Why?",options:[{text:`The default cache key includes the query string, so each campaign tag creates a separate cache object`,explanation:`Correct. Same bytes, different key — one asset shatters into N cold entries, each fetched from origin. Fix: strip or whitelist query parameters in the cache key.`},{text:`Query strings make responses uncacheable per the HTTP spec`,explanation:`They are perfectly cacheable. The issue is key identity, not cacheability.`},{text:`The TTL is reset by the new parameter`,explanation:`TTL is per cached object and unaffected by parameter names. The problem is that new objects are being created at all.`},{text:`The origin is sending Vary: User-Agent`,explanation:`That would also fragment the cache, but it is a different mistake and would not correlate with campaign tags appearing.`}],correct:0},{question:`Fixed window counter, limit 100/min. What is the worst-case burst a client can legally push through?`,options:[{text:`100 requests — the limit is the limit`,explanation:`True per window, but the client can use two adjacent windows back to back.`},{text:`150 requests, because half a window overlaps`,explanation:`The arithmetic is simpler and worse than that — nothing stops a client from filling both windows completely.`},{text:`100 requests plus whatever the sliding window allows`,explanation:`Sliding windows are a different algorithm. Fixed window has no sliding component at all — that is exactly the flaw.`},{text:`200 requests: 100 just before the boundary and 100 just after`,explanation:`Correct. 100 at 12:00:59 plus 100 at 12:01:01 is 200 in a 2-second span — 2x the limit for any minute-long window straddling the reset. This is the boundary problem.`}],correct:3},{question:`Token bucket with capacity 10 and refill 10/s. A client sends a burst of 15 requests in the same millisecond. How many are rejected, and what would fix it?`,options:[{text:`0 rejected — the refill rate matches the burst`,explanation:`Refill happens over time. Within a single instant, zero elapsed time means zero tokens added.`},{text:`5 rejected; only raising capacity helps, because capacity is the burst allowance`,explanation:`Correct. The bucket holds at most 10 tokens, so 10 pass and 5 bounce regardless of refill rate. Capacity governs the instant; refill governs the long run.`},{text:`5 rejected; raising the refill rate to 15/s would fix it`,explanation:`It would not. The burst is instantaneous — no refill rate adds tokens in zero elapsed time. You need capacity 15.`},{text:`15 rejected — a full bucket cannot serve a burst`,explanation:`Backwards: a full bucket is precisely what serves a burst. It serves 10 of them.`}],correct:1},{question:`Your API runs on 10 instances, each enforcing 100 req/min with an in-memory counter. What does a client actually get?`,options:[{text:`100 req/min — the load balancer distributes evenly so the total holds`,explanation:`Each instance counts only what it sees. Ten independent counters of 100 do not sum to one limit of 100.`},{text:`10 req/min — the limit is divided across instances`,explanation:`Nothing divides it. Each instance independently grants a full 100.`},{text:`Up to 1000 req/min — the limit is multiplied by instance count, and autoscaling changes it silently`,explanation:`Correct. N local counters enforce N times the limit. The fix is a shared store (Redis) with an atomic INCR or Lua script; the cost is a network hop per request.`},{text:`It is unpredictable but never exceeds 100 in aggregate`,explanation:`It is indeed unpredictable, but it routinely exceeds 100 — that is the whole problem.`}],correct:2},{question:`A teammate proposes renaming 2 million objects in S3 to reorganize them into a new "folder" structure. What do you say?`,options:[{text:`Fine — it is a metadata operation on the key index`,explanation:`There is no such operation. Object stores have no folder metadata to rewrite.`},{text:`There are no folders and rename is copy-then-delete: this reads and rewrites every byte of 2 million objects`,explanation:`Correct. Keys are flat strings and slashes are a display convention. Each "rename" is a full copy plus a delete — hours of work and real egress cost for zero functional change.`},{text:`It is fine as long as you use a lifecycle rule`,explanation:`Lifecycle rules change storage class or delete objects on a schedule. They do not move keys.`},{text:`Only the listing changes; the bytes stay put`,explanation:`The key IS the address of the bytes. Change the key and the bytes must be rewritten at the new address.`}],correct:1},{question:`An interviewer asks the difference between an API gateway and a service mesh. Best one-liner?`,options:[{text:`The gateway handles north-south traffic entering the system; the mesh handles east-west service-to-service traffic via sidecars`,explanation:`Correct. Overlapping feature lists, different traffic direction — and large systems typically run both.`},{text:`The gateway is for REST, the mesh is for gRPC`,explanation:`Both handle both. Protocol is not the dividing line.`},{text:`The mesh replaces the gateway in modern architectures`,explanation:`A mesh has no public front door, no API-key management, and no external client contract. It does not replace the gateway.`},{text:`The gateway is hardware, the mesh is software`,explanation:`Both are software. This confuses the gateway with older hardware load balancers.`}],correct:0},{question:`Your shared Redis rate-limiter store goes down at peak traffic. Default behaviour for a general public API?`,options:[{text:`Fail closed — reject everything until Redis returns`,explanation:`That converts a limiter outage into a full site outage. Reserve fail-closed for endpoints where abuse beats downtime, like payments or login.`},{text:`Retry the Redis call until it succeeds`,explanation:`Retrying against a down dependency on every request adds latency, exhausts your connection pool, and hammers Redis while it tries to recover.`},{text:`Fall back to per-instance local counters permanently`,explanation:`A reasonable degraded mode, but "permanently" is the bug — you would be enforcing N times the limit with nobody noticing.`},{text:`Fail open, allow the traffic, and alarm loudly`,explanation:`Correct. The limiter must not become the thing that takes the site down. Allow, page someone, and lean on your other protections (autoscaling, circuit breakers) until it recovers.`}],correct:3}],interviewQuestions:[{question:`Case: design the upload path for a video platform. Users upload files up to 2 GB; each video must be transcoded into three resolutions before it goes live.`,answer:`First rule: the bytes must not touch my app servers. The client POSTs metadata to my API — filename, size, content type. The API authorizes, checks quota, generates a storage key, INSERTs a row with status=pending, and returns a pre-signed multipart PUT URL scoped to that key with a 15-minute expiry. The client uploads directly to object storage; at 2 GB multipart matters because it gives per-part retry instead of restarting a failed 2 GB upload. On completion the object store fires an ObjectCreated event to a queue, and a fleet of transcoding workers consumes it, produces three renditions written back as new objects, and updates the row to status=ready with the rendition keys. Playback is served from the CDN, not from origin. Tradeoffs I would volunteer: (1) no transaction spans the DB row and the object, so I need a janitor job reconciling pending rows with no object and orphan objects with no row; (2) the queue is what lets a launch-day spike wait instead of melting the workers, at the cost of variable time-to-ready — so the UI shows a processing state; (3) the pre-signed URL is a bearer credential, so it is short-lived, single-key, and single-method. If the interviewer pushes on the naive design: proxying 1000 concurrent 50 MB uploads is ~1.3 Gbps in and out through my app tier, and no amount of CPU fixes a saturated NIC.`,isCaseBased:!0},{question:`What is a pre-signed URL, and what exactly does it protect?`,answer:`A pre-signed URL is an ordinary object-storage URL with an expiry and an HMAC signature in the query string, generated by a party that holds storage credentials. The object store verifies the signature locally, so possession of the URL is the authorization — no callback to my API. It is scoped: one bucket, one key, one HTTP method, one time window. What it protects is my app tier from being in the data path, and my storage credentials from being on the client. What it does not protect: the URL is a bearer token, so anyone who obtains it within the window can use it — which is why expiries are minutes, not days, why they are always over HTTPS, and why upload URLs are scoped to a key my server chose rather than one the client supplied (otherwise a client can overwrite someone else's object). For content type and size enforcement, the signed policy can pin those too, because the client is otherwise free to PUT whatever it likes at that key.`,isCaseBased:!1},{question:`Why is an object store not a filesystem, and where does that bite in a design?`,answer:`Four differences that matter. There are no directories — keys are flat strings and the slashes are a UI convention. Rename is copy-then-delete, so "reorganize the folder structure" on a million objects is a million full-object copies. There is no partial append or in-place edit; you replace the whole object, which kills the idea of appending to a log file in a bucket. And list is a paged query over a key index, not a directory read — a bucket with 50M objects makes list slow and costly. Where it bites: designs that use the bucket as their index ("list the prefix to find a user's files") work at 100 objects and die at 10 million. The fix is the standard split — the queryable index lives in the database, the bytes live at keys in the store. And when I need small random reads, POSIX semantics, or locking, an object store is simply the wrong tool.`,isCaseBased:!1},{question:`Case: overnight your CDN hit ratio fell from 96% to 62% and origin load went up 6x. Walk me through the debug.`,answer:`The traffic did not change, so the cache key or the TTLs did. I would pull the CDN log fields for cache status and cache key and group misses by URL to see whether one path dominates. The usual suspects, in order: (1) a query parameter now varies — new campaign tags, a cache-busting timestamp someone added, or a client library appending a request id — so identical bytes get distinct keys; (2) a Vary header changed, most destructively Vary: User-Agent, which fragments every object by browser version; (3) a deploy shortened max-age or started sending Cache-Control: no-store on assets; (4) a mass purge left every PoP cold and the herd is still refilling. The fixes match: whitelist or strip query parameters in the cache key, remove the bad Vary, restore TTLs, and move to versioned immutable filenames so future deploys never require a purge at all. Meanwhile I would protect the origin right now — enable origin shield or request coalescing so N simultaneous misses for the same object become one origin fetch, and, if the origin is genuinely at risk, serve stale-while-revalidate rather than let it fall over.`,isCaseBased:!0},{question:`What does a CDN buy beyond latency, and what does it cost you?`,answer:`Beyond latency it buys three things. Origin offload: at a 95% hit ratio the origin sees 1 request in 20, so both the fleet size and the egress bill shrink about 20x, and CDN egress is cheaper per GB than cloud egress on top of that. DDoS absorption: hundreds of PoPs with terabits of aggregate capacity dissipate a volumetric flood that would flatten a single-region origin, and TLS termination plus WAF rules usually sit at the same edge. And a security perimeter for private content via signed URLs, so paid media is cached near the user yet not publicly shareable. The costs are the ones every cache has, amplified: staleness you cannot fully control, invalidation that is best-effort and takes seconds-to-minutes across PoPs, negative caching that pins a 500 in place after you fix it, and the catastrophic failure mode of caching a personalized response under a shared key. Plus a vendor dependency and a debugging surface you cannot inspect directly. I would say plainly that a CDN is a caching layer, so every tradeoff from the caching module applies here, multiplied by the number of PoPs.`,isCaseBased:!1},{question:`An API gateway centralizes auth, rate limiting, routing, and logging. When does that become a liability?`,answer:`Centralizing cross-cutting concerns is right: thirty services each implementing JWT validation is thirty chances to get it wrong, and one place that sees every request is where your SLO dashboard and per-customer billing data come from. It becomes a liability when business logic migrates into it — conditional routing on payload fields, response assembly, per-customer special cases in config. Then it is a smart pipe: a shared, undertested deployment bottleneck that every team queues behind, owned by nobody, and a single point of failure adding a hop of latency. My rule is generic concerns in the gateway, product logic in services. If one client type keeps demanding shaped responses, that is a BFF — a separate gateway per client, so a mobile app's desire for one aggregated call never distorts the shared API. And I would run the gateway redundantly with the same rigor as any service, because everything is behind it.`,isCaseBased:!1},{question:`Token bucket, leaky bucket, fixed window, sliding window log, sliding window counter — pick one and defend it.`,answer:`Token bucket by default. Two numbers per key (token count and last-refill timestamp), refill computed lazily as min(capacity, tokens + elapsed * rate) so there is no background timer, and it allows the bursts real clients actually produce — a page load fires twenty requests then goes quiet. Capacity is the burst allowance, refill rate is the sustained ceiling; those two knobs express almost every policy I need. Leaky bucket only when the downstream literally cannot absorb a burst — a legacy system or a paid API with a hard per-second cap — because smoothing the output costs queueing latency and a queue to size and monitor. Fixed window when I need the absolute cheapest thing and I can live with 2x at the boundary. Sliding window log when the limit must be exact and the limit value is small, since memory is one timestamp per allowed request per key. Sliding window counter when I want near-exactness at two integers per key — the previous window's count weighted by overlap plus the current one — which is what I would ship for a large public API.`,isCaseBased:!1},{question:`Show me the arithmetic behind the fixed-window boundary problem, and how the sliding window counter fixes it.`,answer:`Limit 100 per minute, windows resetting on the minute. A client sends 100 requests at 12:00:59 — legal, that window is now full — and 100 more at 12:01:01, also legal in the fresh window. That is 200 requests inside a 2-second span, and for any 60-second window straddling 12:01:00 the client got twice its limit. The sliding window counter fixes it by refusing to forget the previous window: it keeps both counts and weights the previous by how much of the current sliding window still overlaps it. At 12:01:15 with a 60-second window, 45 of those 60 seconds lie in the previous minute, so the estimate is prev * 45/60 + current — with prev=80 and current=20 that is 60 + 20 = 80, under the limit, allow. Cost: two integers per key instead of one. The only inaccuracy is the assumption that the previous window's traffic was uniformly distributed, which makes the estimate slightly off in either direction but bounded, and in practice far better than a 2x cliff.`,isCaseBased:!1},{question:`You deploy the limiter as a local in-memory token bucket on each app instance. What breaks, and what is the fix and its cost?`,answer:`The limit gets multiplied by the instance count. Ten instances each granting 100 req/min means the client gets up to 1000, and autoscaling to forty quadruples the published limit with nobody changing a config. Dividing the limit by N is the tempting fix and it fails twice: load balancers are not perfectly even, so a client whose requests land unevenly hits an artificially low limit, and any instance death or scale event changes everyone's effective quota. The real fix is a shared store — Redis — with an atomic operation: INCR plus EXPIRE for counters, or a small Lua script that does read, refill, decrement, write as one indivisible step for a token bucket. Atomicity is not optional; without it two instances read the same token count and both spend it, and the limit leaks under exactly the load you built it for. The cost is a network round trip per request, ~0.5–1 ms in-datacenter, which is a 5% tax on a 20 ms endpoint and 50% on a 2 ms one. Mitigations: same-AZ Redis, pipelining, sharding the limiter by key, or going approximate with local buckets that sync every few hundred milliseconds. And I would decide fail-open versus fail-closed up front — fail open with an alarm for a general API, fail closed for login and payments.`,isCaseBased:!1},{question:`What should a rate limiter key on? Why is IP alone wrong?`,answer:`User ID or API key for authenticated traffic: it is the accountable unit, it survives network changes, and it maps to the plan the customer bought. IP is what remains for anonymous endpoints — login, signup, password reset — but IP alone is wrong as a general policy because of NAT. An office, a university, or a mobile carrier's carrier-grade NAT puts thousands of real users behind one address, so a limit tight enough to stop an attacker locks out an entire ISP's customers, and a limit loose enough to spare them stops nobody. IPv6 inverts it: attackers get a free /64, so limit on the prefix rather than the address. The real design is layered — per-user for fairness, per-IP or per-prefix for anonymous abuse, per-endpoint because login deserves 5/min while a cached read deserves 1000/min, and a global limit that protects backend capacity regardless of who is calling. One security detail: if I key on IP behind a proxy I must take the client address from a trusted position in the forwarded chain, because a naively trusted X-Forwarded-For makes the whole limiter bypassable.`,isCaseBased:!1},{question:`Case: you run an LLM API where each request costs you about $0.02 in inference. One customer on the $100/month plan is sending 500,000 requests a month. Design the controls.`,answer:`That customer costs $10,000 against $100 of revenue, so this is a pricing and metering problem that a rate limiter enforces. Three layers. First, a quota — a monthly budget in tokens or requests per API key, tracked in a shared counter, because a per-second rate limit alone never caps a monthly bill. Second, a token bucket per key for the rate: capacity as the burst allowance so a batch job is not punished for parallelism, refill as the sustained ceiling that maps to the plan tier. Third, a global backend limit so no combination of customers can exceed my inference capacity — that one protects availability, not margin. Enforcement lives at the gateway keyed on API key, with the counters in Redis behind an atomic Lua script, since local per-instance counters would multiply every limit by my fleet size. The response contract matters here because callers are machines: 429 with Retry-After, plus RateLimit-Limit/Remaining/Reset on every response so a good client self-paces and never gets rejected. Graceful degradation is a real option instead of a hard 429 — route over-quota traffic to a smaller, cheaper model, or accept it as a batch job returning 202 with a job id at lower priority. Tradeoff I would name: metering by request is easy but wrong for LLMs, since a 200,000-token prompt is not one request's worth of cost — the limiter should decrement by estimated tokens and reconcile against actual usage after the call.`,isCaseBased:!0},{question:`Case: an interviewer says "traffic tripled after a product launch and everything is slow." You may add exactly one piece of infrastructure from this module. Which, and why?`,answer:`I would ask what fraction of the traffic is static or cacheable first, because the answer decides between two candidates. If the load is mostly reads of the same content — images, scripts, product pages, media — a CDN is the single highest-leverage addition: at a 95% hit ratio the origin sees one request in twenty, so tripled traffic becomes less origin load than before the launch, and the same edge absorbs the bandwidth cost and any opportunistic attack. If instead the traffic is genuinely unique authenticated requests, a CDN buys nothing and the answer is rate limiting at the gateway — not to block customers, but to shed the abusive and runaway share predictably, so the system degrades in an order I chose. I would name what I am deliberately not choosing: more app instances is the reflex answer, but it just moves the bottleneck to the database, and it is the expensive fix if 90% of the load was cacheable. The follow-up I would expect is "what if the slow part is uploads?" — then neither: it is the pre-signed URL change, because proxying freight through app servers saturates NICs and no amount of horizontal scaling fixes bandwidth you did not need to spend.`,isCaseBased:!0}],flashcards:[{front:`Blob store: three strengths and three things it is NOT`,back:`Strengths: cheap (~$0.023/GB-mo), effectively unlimited, durable via replication across 3+ AZs, HTTP-addressable. NOT a filesystem: no directories (flat keys), rename = full copy, no partial append or in-place edit, list is a paged query.`},{front:`The pre-signed upload pattern, one breath`,back:`Client asks the API for a pre-signed URL (auth + quota + pending row) → client PUTs bytes DIRECTLY to storage → storage fires an event → queue → worker processes and flips status to ready. Your app servers never carry the freight.`},{front:`Standard media data model`,back:`Metadata in the database (id, owner, size, content_type, status, storage key); bytes in the blob store. No transaction spans them — reconcile with a status column and a janitor job.`},{front:`Cache-hit ratio arithmetic, and the thing that wrecks it`,back:`10,000 req/s at the edge: 95% hit → 500/s at origin; 70% hit → 3,000/s (a 6x spike from a config change). The usual cause: the default cache key includes the full query string, so ?utm_source=X makes a distinct object per campaign tag. Fix: strip/whitelist query params; Vary only on Accept-Encoding, never User-Agent.`},{front:`Purge vs versioned URLs`,back:`Purge must reach every PoP — seconds to minutes, rate-limited, and a cold cache stampedes the origin. Versioned immutable filenames (/app.9f3c2a.js, max-age=1y) mean nothing ever needs invalidating.`},{front:`API gateway: contents, failure mode, and its neighbours`,back:`In it: routing, authN/authZ, rate limiting, transformation, API-key management, metrics/logging. Failure mode: business logic creeps in and it becomes a smart pipe — a shared deployment bottleneck and single point of failure. Gateway = north-south; service mesh = east-west via sidecars; BFF = one gateway per client type.`},{front:`Token bucket: the two numbers`,back:`Capacity = burst allowance (decides how big an instantaneous burst survives). Refill rate = sustained ceiling (long-run req/s). Lazy refill: tokens = min(capacity, tokens + elapsed * rate). Two numbers per key, O(1).`},{front:`The four other limiter algorithms`,back:`Leaky bucket: FIFO drains at constant rate, smooths output, adds latency. Fixed window: one counter, but 2x at the boundary (100 at 12:00:59 + 100 at 12:01:01). Sliding log: exact, one timestamp per request. Sliding counter: prev*overlap + cur — two ints, the practical ship.`},{front:`The distributed rate-limit trap`,back:`N instances with local counters enforce N x the limit (10 instances x 100/min = 1000/min), and autoscaling changes it silently. Fix: shared Redis with an atomic INCR or Lua script. Cost: ~0.5–1 ms network hop per request.`},{front:`The 429 contract + what to key on`,back:`429 + Retry-After, plus X-RateLimit-Limit/Remaining/Reset on every response; client backs off exponentially WITH jitter (no jitter = retry storm). Key on user/API key; IP alone is wrong (NAT hides thousands behind one address; on IPv6 limit the /64).`}],mindmapMarkdown:`- Blob Storage, CDNs, API Gateways & Rate Limiting
  - Blob / object storage
    - Cheap, unlimited, 3+ AZ replication, HTTP-addressable
    - NOT a filesystem: flat keys, rename = copy, no append, list is a query
    - Metadata in DB, bytes in the store (no cross-transaction)
    - Storage classes + lifecycle rules; egress is the real bill
  - Pre-signed upload pattern
    - Never proxy uploads through the app server
    - 1000 x 50 MB in 5 min = ~1.3 Gbps in AND out
    - API returns 400 bytes (auth, quota, key, signed URL); client PUTs direct
    - Storage event to queue to worker to status=ready
  - CDN
    - PoPs at the edge; ~200 ms origin vs ~5 ms edge
    - Cache-hit ratio: 95% = 20x origin offload
    - Cache key: query params and Vary shatter the cache
    - TTL; purge is slow across PoPs, versioned immutable URLs win
    - Push (known hot set) vs pull (default, cold misses); signed URLs for private
    - Beyond speed: origin offload, DDoS absorption, TLS/WAF
    - It IS a cache: staleness, negative caching, never cache personalized
  - API gateway
    - Routing, authN/authZ, rate limiting, transformation, API keys, metrics
    - Risk: the smart pipe, a shared deployment bottleneck
    - BFF per client; gateway = north-south, mesh = east-west
  - Rate limiting
    - Why: protect backend, tenant fairness, abuse, cost control
    - Token bucket: capacity = burst, refill = sustained ceiling
    - Leaky bucket: constant output, queued bursts, added latency
    - Fixed window cheap but 2x at the boundary; sliding log exact but memory-hungry
    - Sliding counter = prev x overlap + cur, two ints, what everyone ships
    - Where: edge/gateway coarse, per-service fine
    - Distributed: N instances = N x limit; atomic Redis, a network hop, fail open
    - Key on user/API key; IP alone breaks behind NAT, /64 on IPv6
    - 429 + Retry-After + X-RateLimit-*; jittered backoff or degrade (stale, 202)`};export{e as default};